        {
          "published_at": "2026-11-12T09:21:00Z",
          "url": "https://x.com/thorstenball/status/2120809071510970414",
          "topic": "Team sizing and organizational change with AI",
          "text": "What's the right team size?\n\nProbably not the one you set in 2024.\n\nThink about it: your infrastructure team was 8 people back then, but now, with each person able to do so much more, faster, is 8 still the right size?\n\nDo you have more infrastructure to manage? If you have more infrastructure, then maybe. If not, you no longer need 8 people on that team.\n\nIf the the ambitions of the company align with the increased productivity of teams, that's great, of course. But not every team in every company needs to scale up. \n\nIf your business success isn't a function of how much infrastructure you manage (to keep using that example), then you want to keep the amount of work the infra team does fixed. And that amount requires less people now.\n\nProduct teams, on the other hand, can now do a lot more and very likely should. But there the question is: does the org allow that?\n\nI was talking to @lewis_b_metcalf yesterday about some engineers really crashing out and lamenting the loss of craft.\n\nHe said: \"Yeah, I get it. If you're in a company where all you were hired for and all you're allowed to do is engineering, then things might suck. But here [at Amp], I can do marketing, I can think of new product ideas, I can ship them, talk to users, record videos. I can do so much more and it's the most fun I ever had.\"\n\nThat's, I think, the crux of the matter: your engineers won't become 10x more effective if your org doesn't allow them to.\n\nIf you haven't done this four times this year already, it's high time to rethink all of your org boundaries and roles.\n\nOf course AI won't have an impact on your business if your engineers are sitting there, twiddling their thumbs, waiting for someone to hand them a ticket.",
          "quote_tweet": null,
          "author_replies": [],
          "render_audit": {
